<?xml version="1.0"?>
<model>
  <name>continuum_arm_pcc</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <sdf version='1.9'>model.sdf</sdf>

  <author>
   <name>Aabhash Bhandari</name>
  </author>

  <description>
    3-tendon soft continuum robotic arm: Ø40mm OD / Ø30mm ID lattice-TPU
    tube (behaves like a traditional silicone continuum arm), mounted on a
    cylindrical adapter base (radius 0.04m, height 0.02m). The 0.40m arm is
    discretized into 15 rigid segments (~26.67mm each, solid-cylinder
    visual/collision at the 20mm outer radius but with mass/inertia computed
    from the true hollow-tube cross-section), connected by 2-DOF universal
    joints (X/Y bending). Passive elastic compliance is modeled with native
    gz-sim joint spring dynamics (K_theta = E*I/l_s = 1.611 N*m/rad,
    damping=0.08 - derived from the SAME Young's modulus and true hollow-tube
    second moment of area continuum_arm_cosserat's solver uses for its Kbt,
    so both models represent physically consistent material behavior) on
    every joint axis. Segments are shaded in a navy-to-cyan blue gradient
    (base to tip) purely for visual identification of the arm in a composed
    scene.

    Material: E=5e5 Pa, G=1.6e5 Pa for the lattice-infill TPU tube wall
    (effective density ~780 kg/m^3, ~65% of solid TPU's ~1200 kg/m^3). An
    Ashby bending-dominated-lattice estimate (E_eff ~= E_solid*rel_density^2)
    with a soft printable TPU (E_solid~1.2MPa) at this relative density lands
    almost exactly on 5e5 Pa, consistent with "behaves like a silicone soft
    arm" (typical silicone Young's modulus is 0.05-0.5 MPa).

    Mass budget: total assembly (base + 15 segments) is ~250g (m_base~78.4g
    + 15*m_seg~11.4g), using a uniform effective density of ~780 kg/m^3
    across base and tube alike - see gen_pcc_arm.py.

    Actuated by the TendonArmController model plugin (see
    plugins/tendon_arm_controller), which subscribes to a 3-tendon length
    command (gz.msgs.Vector3d on /continuum_arm_pcc/tendon_cmd, x=l1,
    y=l2, z=l3, 3 tendons routed along the inner wall's 30mm-diameter
    circle, spaced 120deg apart at pitch radius d=0.015m = ID/2), maps it to
    constant-curvature target bend angles (theta_x, theta_y) via the
    standard 3-tendon Jacobian, distributes theta_x/N and theta_y/N as the
    per-joint target, and drives every joint toward that target with a PD
    force loop layered on top of the passive spring dynamics.

    Tendons are pulled by a 35kgcm servo (3.43 N*m stall torque) - through
    any realistic winch-drum radius this delivers well over 100N of tendon
    tension, so the servo is never the binding actuation constraint for this
    arm. The PD force loop's effort_limit (0.24 N*m = continuum_arm_cosserat's
    max_tension * pitch_radius = 16N * 0.015m) is a shared servo-derived
    torque ceiling applied to both models' joints, not a reachability
    limit for PCC's own kinematic model - unlike continuum_arm_cosserat,
    PCC has no notion of axial tendon load or column buckling at all, so
    its reachable single-tendon bend range is set by the commanded
    tendon-length magnitude, not by this cap. See
    continuum_arm_cosserat/model.config for why Cosserat's own reachable
    range is smaller (a genuine buckling limit of this material/geometry,
    not an arbitrary clamp).

    Joint state (actual, physics-integrated angles) is published via the
    stock JointStatePublisher system on /continuum_arm_pcc/joint_states.

    Mount contract: continuum_arm_pcc/base_cyl's top face (z=0 in this
    module's own frame) is the attachment interface, meant for a
    bottom-mounted glue joint from a composing vehicle model (e.g.
    hillstar), the same pattern rigid_arm_3link uses - see that model's
    header comment for the reasoning.
  </description>
</model>
